WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:08.550 --> 00:00:13.138
Through film and media, we
can learn about empathy.

00:00:13.138 --> 00:00:17.017
Empathy is something we
really need right now.

00:00:17.017 --> 00:00:20.687
Story and art has the power to
change things in the world

00:00:20.687 --> 00:00:23.023
and make the world
a better place.

00:00:23.023 --> 00:00:26.902
It's giving people a relief  from
their lives, for a couple of hours.

00:00:26.902 --> 00:00:29.112
I think it's up to the
filmmakers to do what's right,

00:00:29.112 --> 00:00:33.158
using their influence
for the greater good.

00:00:33.158 --> 00:00:35.327
If you have seen
my film, what I'm

00:00:35.327 --> 00:00:40.249
really looking for as
generic universal language.

00:00:40.249 --> 00:00:41.792
Anyone can watch this film.

00:00:41.792 --> 00:00:44.169
And put themselves in
our character's shoes.

00:00:44.169 --> 00:00:46.338
And I think that's beautiful.

00:00:46.338 --> 00:00:48.966
We are doing this
film to bring change.

00:00:48.966 --> 00:00:51.593
This kind of crime should
never happen again.

00:00:51.593 --> 00:00:55.681
I want people to look critically
how that these stories are

00:00:55.681 --> 00:00:56.640
still going on.

00:00:56.640 --> 00:00:58.016
Really looking at
introspectively

00:00:58.016 --> 00:01:00.143
and thinking what
is wrong with that.

00:01:00.143 --> 00:01:02.271
I wanted the
audience to take away

00:01:02.271 --> 00:01:05.315
how they affect the
environment and nature

00:01:05.315 --> 00:01:07.484
with a cup of coffee.

00:01:07.484 --> 00:01:08.819
What is your role in the world?

00:01:08.819 --> 00:01:10.279
To what extent you
have an impact?

00:01:10.279 --> 00:01:12.489
And how can you do better?

00:01:12.489 --> 00:01:15.909
I want them to ask who
was the real culprit.

00:01:15.909 --> 00:01:18.120
I want to raise awareness.

00:01:18.120 --> 00:01:20.205
I think what's so
important to me

00:01:20.205 --> 00:01:23.625
is to expose the world
to South African stories,

00:01:23.625 --> 00:01:26.044
to South African talent.

00:01:26.044 --> 00:01:28.922
It will be important for me
to get people to just leave

00:01:28.922 --> 00:01:31.925
with an appreciation for
these kids who are far more

00:01:31.925 --> 00:01:35.762
resilient and smarter and
have a very real sense of what

00:01:35.762 --> 00:01:37.097
life is.

00:01:37.097 --> 00:01:41.143
In these times
filmmaking helps us heal.

00:01:41.143 --> 00:01:43.020
I don't think
filmmaking is important.

00:01:43.020 --> 00:01:45.605
I think it's essential
for our lives.

00:01:49.860 --> 00:01:50.944
Welcome, everyone.

00:01:50.944 --> 00:01:53.405
I'm David Rubin, president of
the Academy of Motion Picture

00:01:53.405 --> 00:01:55.157
Arts and Sciences.

00:01:55.157 --> 00:01:58.201
It's my honor to introduce
the first ever virtual Student

00:01:58.201 --> 00:01:59.786
Academy Awards.

00:01:59.786 --> 00:02:01.705
Although I'm hopeful,
as we all are,

00:02:01.705 --> 00:02:05.876
that this will also be the last
virtual Student Academy Awards.

00:02:05.876 --> 00:02:09.296
Just as inflection
points like the Cold War

00:02:09.296 --> 00:02:12.424
and 9/11 shaped the
perspective of past generations

00:02:12.424 --> 00:02:17.262
of filmmakers from Stanley
Kramer to Kathryn Bigelow,

00:02:17.262 --> 00:02:19.639
2020, a year like no
other, will no doubt

00:02:19.639 --> 00:02:21.516
forever influence
what your generation

00:02:21.516 --> 00:02:23.769
sees through the camera lens.

00:02:23.769 --> 00:02:26.605
And judging by this year's
nearly 1,500 submissions

00:02:26.605 --> 00:02:30.067
received from 328
schools from Albania

00:02:30.067 --> 00:02:34.571
to Vietnam, what a promising
generation you are.

00:02:34.571 --> 00:02:37.741
My thanks go to the more
than 750 Academy members

00:02:37.741 --> 00:02:39.743
who volunteered their
time to judge entries

00:02:39.743 --> 00:02:42.371
in seven categories
and had the challenging

00:02:42.371 --> 00:02:45.832
responsibility of selecting
this year's gold, silver,

00:02:45.832 --> 00:02:47.042
and bronze winners.

00:02:47.042 --> 00:02:49.878
And now I'm pleased to introduce
someone whose career trajectory

00:02:49.878 --> 00:02:53.674
from Mexico City to Hollywood is
an inspiring example of carving

00:02:53.674 --> 00:02:54.758
one's own path.

00:02:54.758 --> 00:02:57.094
Recently he starred in
and executive produced

00:02:57.094 --> 00:02:59.137
Dora and the Lost City of Gold.

00:02:59.137 --> 00:03:01.682
Please welcome the host of
this year's Student Academy

00:03:01.682 --> 00:03:06.061
Awards, actor, director, and
producer, Eugenio Derbez.

00:03:06.061 --> 00:03:07.437
Thank you, David.

00:03:07.437 --> 00:03:09.189
Hello, everyone.

00:03:09.189 --> 00:03:12.526
Even though I'm the one
on your screen right now,

00:03:12.526 --> 00:03:16.154
this night is for the people
who deserve the credit, you,

00:03:16.154 --> 00:03:19.449
the nominees and recipients
of this year's awards,

00:03:19.449 --> 00:03:21.743
who earned the applause
that we're sending

00:03:21.743 --> 00:03:23.370
across the internet to you.

00:03:25.956 --> 00:03:26.456
Bravo.

00:03:26.456 --> 00:03:31.336
Over time, Hollywood has become
more inclusive, more open,

00:03:31.336 --> 00:03:32.921
more accepting.

00:03:32.921 --> 00:03:35.340
But we still have a ways to go.

00:03:35.340 --> 00:03:37.342
To make it in
Hollywood is not easy.

00:03:37.342 --> 00:03:40.137
But however, at its
best, our industry

00:03:40.137 --> 00:03:43.432
can be that shining
example that there is

00:03:43.432 --> 00:03:45.600
room at the table for everyone.

00:03:45.600 --> 00:03:47.894
This year, we couldn't
be on a stage.

00:03:47.894 --> 00:03:52.441
But in previous years, we
couldn't or wouldn't do

00:03:52.441 --> 00:03:54.276
what just happened a week ago.

00:03:54.276 --> 00:03:58.530
We invited this year's students
to be part of a Zoom interview

00:03:58.530 --> 00:04:01.741
and asked them how it
felt to be a finalist.

00:04:01.741 --> 00:04:05.245
And then, listen, and
then, we surprised them

00:04:05.245 --> 00:04:08.165
by beaming in legends
from their fields,

00:04:08.165 --> 00:04:10.792
who then revealed the
real news to each winner.

00:04:10.792 --> 00:04:14.379
So let's get to
what really matters,

00:04:14.379 --> 00:04:18.467
the spectacular artists who
are all winners in tonight's

00:04:18.467 --> 00:04:22.721
first category, animation
and international animation.

00:04:22.721 --> 00:04:24.723
Every year, the
winners in this field

00:04:24.723 --> 00:04:29.102
find mesmerizing new ways
to transform pen and ink

00:04:29.102 --> 00:04:31.396
into pixels and plasma screens.

00:04:31.396 --> 00:04:34.024
And your new generation
promises to push us

00:04:34.024 --> 00:04:36.735
towards farther frontiers.

00:04:36.735 --> 00:04:39.321
This year's domestic
animation winners

00:04:39.321 --> 00:04:43.950
had the joy of being given the
big news by an Oscar-winning

00:04:43.950 --> 00:04:47.204
Disney legend and animation
director of the soon

00:04:47.204 --> 00:04:52.375
to be released Over
the Moon, Glenn Keane.

00:04:52.375 --> 00:04:55.003
It's a real pleasure to
introduce you to Glenn Keane.

00:04:57.672 --> 00:04:58.924
Oh, my gosh.

00:04:58.924 --> 00:04:59.841
Hi.

00:04:59.841 --> 00:05:01.468
Such an honor to meet you.

00:05:01.468 --> 00:05:05.597
Sorry, you're like, my hero.

00:05:05.597 --> 00:05:06.640
Thank you, Kate.

00:05:06.640 --> 00:05:10.310
Daniela, Chrisy,
Maya, congratulations.

00:05:10.310 --> 00:05:15.273
You have won the bronze medal
for your beautiful, beautiful

00:05:15.273 --> 00:05:16.358
film, Hamsa.

00:05:16.358 --> 00:05:17.400
Thank you.

00:05:17.400 --> 00:05:19.778
Thank you so much.

00:05:19.778 --> 00:05:21.988
It's such an honor to meet you.

00:05:21.988 --> 00:05:24.449
Anyway, I'm fighting
back tears right now.

00:05:24.449 --> 00:05:26.660
This is amazing.

00:05:26.660 --> 00:05:28.995
Our story is about
a little girl.

00:05:28.995 --> 00:05:32.207
Her mother tells her to fear
the other or the unknown.

00:05:32.207 --> 00:05:35.502
And she soon learns that
actually, we're all the same.

00:05:35.502 --> 00:05:38.088
And that there is no
reason to fear each other.

00:05:38.088 --> 00:05:40.590
I feel, like, anyone
could watch this film.

00:05:40.590 --> 00:05:42.843
And put themselves into
our character's shoes.

00:05:42.843 --> 00:05:45.887
Oka, it was a very
meaningful story.

00:05:45.887 --> 00:05:48.765
We can change any
generation's mindset.

00:05:48.765 --> 00:05:50.267
I think it's very
important to show

00:05:50.267 --> 00:05:52.060
different cultures on screen.

00:05:52.060 --> 00:05:55.522
Us as a team, we have so many
countries that we represent.

00:05:55.522 --> 00:05:59.234
It just proves to you that we
can go past what we look like.

00:05:59.234 --> 00:06:02.946
I just want to thank first and
foremost my thesis partners.

00:06:02.946 --> 00:06:06.575
I also really want to thank
my family and friends who

00:06:06.575 --> 00:06:09.411
knew that I could do it
more than I even knew.

00:06:09.411 --> 00:06:12.998
I would love to thank my thesis
advisers, Harry Dorrington

00:06:12.998 --> 00:06:16.334
Elizabeth Ku-Herrero
Eric Cunha and SVA

00:06:16.334 --> 00:06:19.337
for supporting us through
our journey and difficulties.

00:06:19.337 --> 00:06:22.465
We definitely could not
have done it on our own.

00:06:22.465 --> 00:06:24.509
I would just like
to thank my family.

00:06:24.509 --> 00:06:27.554
I love you guys so much, all
my friends from back home.

00:06:27.554 --> 00:06:28.847
Thank you again to the Academy.

00:06:28.847 --> 00:06:30.932
This is honestly so amazing.

00:06:30.932 --> 00:06:34.311
So thank you.

00:06:34.311 --> 00:06:38.148
It's an honor to tell you, you
have won the silver medal award

00:06:38.148 --> 00:06:41.568
for beautiful, beautiful
film, Mime Your Manners.

00:06:41.568 --> 00:06:42.736
Thank you so much.

00:06:42.736 --> 00:06:44.738
It's amazing to
hear it from you.

00:06:44.738 --> 00:06:46.406
I wish I could give
you a hug right now.

00:06:46.406 --> 00:06:47.866
I'll give you a virtual hug.

00:06:50.744 --> 00:06:52.912
Our film is about
an arrogant man that

00:06:52.912 --> 00:06:54.748
is transformed into a mime.

00:06:54.748 --> 00:06:57.375
And then he has to grow
to be a better person

00:06:57.375 --> 00:06:58.668
to be freed from it.

00:06:58.668 --> 00:07:00.670
This story was important
to us because we

00:07:00.670 --> 00:07:04.007
wanted to tell a
lesson about anger,

00:07:04.007 --> 00:07:07.552
and controlling that anger,
and how you want to be treated,

00:07:07.552 --> 00:07:09.054
and how you treat others.

00:07:09.054 --> 00:07:10.930
I hope this story
resonates with other people

00:07:10.930 --> 00:07:13.516
because it's about
the golden rule.

00:07:13.516 --> 00:07:16.019
And to show that even
if someone at one point

00:07:16.019 --> 00:07:18.647
is hateful and angry,
that they can grow

00:07:18.647 --> 00:07:20.440
and to understand each other.

00:07:20.440 --> 00:07:23.568
Our film, Mime your Manners
won the Student Academy Award.

00:07:23.568 --> 00:07:24.861
Oh, my goodness.

00:07:24.861 --> 00:07:26.154
What?

00:07:26.154 --> 00:07:26.946
Yeah.

00:07:26.946 --> 00:07:30.075
[APPLAUSE]

00:07:32.285 --> 00:07:35.622
I want to thank my mom and
dad, my brother and sister,

00:07:35.622 --> 00:07:38.917
and the rest of my family for
being so loving and supportive.

00:07:38.917 --> 00:07:41.211
I'd like to thank my older
brother Keith, my parents,

00:07:41.211 --> 00:07:41.920
and my family.

00:07:41.920 --> 00:07:46.591
And last but not least, my
significant other, Rebecca.

00:07:46.591 --> 00:07:50.970
Your film, Ciervo, is
absolutely beautiful.

00:07:50.970 --> 00:07:55.767
You have won the gold for
the Student Academy Award.

00:07:55.767 --> 00:07:57.185
Thank you so much.

00:07:57.185 --> 00:07:57.936
Here you go.

00:07:57.936 --> 00:07:58.687
There you go.

00:07:58.687 --> 00:08:01.064
Go ahead and take it

00:08:01.064 --> 00:08:04.693
My film is Ciervo,
about a young girl

00:08:04.693 --> 00:08:07.362
grappling with an abusive,
violent relationship

00:08:07.362 --> 00:08:09.531
and also comparing
that relationship

00:08:09.531 --> 00:08:11.783
to deer and predator-prey
relationships

00:08:11.783 --> 00:08:13.118
in the environment.

00:08:13.118 --> 00:08:15.954
What I want people to take
away is an understanding

00:08:15.954 --> 00:08:18.498
of the difficult situations
that are portrayed

00:08:18.498 --> 00:08:21.042
and feelings of empathy
for a lot of the characters

00:08:21.042 --> 00:08:23.753
that maybe you wouldn't
normally have empathy for.

00:08:23.753 --> 00:08:26.756
I always feel the best
way to express something

00:08:26.756 --> 00:08:29.676
for me is just visuals
and drawings and images,

00:08:29.676 --> 00:08:32.345
even if it's images that
don't really mean anything.

00:08:32.345 --> 00:08:34.723
[SPEAKING SPANISH]

00:08:47.777 --> 00:08:50.030
I'd like to thank the
Academy for giving me

00:08:50.030 --> 00:08:53.992
such a wonderful opportunity and
giving me this amazing award.

00:08:53.992 --> 00:08:56.369
I feel truly humbled
and honored to be one

00:08:56.369 --> 00:08:58.163
of the recipients this year.

00:08:58.163 --> 00:09:01.291
I encourage people to share
themselves and share their work

00:09:01.291 --> 00:09:03.793
and share each other's
work and connect to it

00:09:03.793 --> 00:09:04.753
and talk about it.

00:09:04.753 --> 00:09:06.629
Be passionate and driven.

00:09:06.629 --> 00:09:09.799
As long as you have a goal,
and you're passionate about it,

00:09:09.799 --> 00:09:10.925
you can do anything.

00:09:10.925 --> 00:09:13.344
Thank you to the
Student Academy Awards

00:09:13.344 --> 00:09:15.722
for this amazing opportunity.

00:09:15.722 --> 00:09:19.601
And hopefully,
we'll see you later.

00:09:19.601 --> 00:09:22.020
Congratulations to
our animation winners.

00:09:22.020 --> 00:09:24.564
Also, our international
animation winner

00:09:24.564 --> 00:09:28.318
was surprised by the amazingly
talented Japanese artist

00:09:28.318 --> 00:09:30.695
and filmmaker who
most recently gave us

00:09:30.695 --> 00:09:35.825
the mesmerizing experimental
film, Leaking Life, Shunsaku

00:09:35.825 --> 00:09:37.994
Hayashi.

00:09:37.994 --> 00:09:41.414
Your film won the gold medal
in the international animation

00:09:41.414 --> 00:09:41.915
category.

00:09:41.915 --> 00:09:43.166
Congratulations.

00:09:43.166 --> 00:09:43.875
No.

00:09:43.875 --> 00:09:44.375
No.

00:09:44.375 --> 00:09:45.126
No way.

00:09:45.126 --> 00:09:45.627
No.

00:09:45.627 --> 00:09:48.129
This is not, no,
no, this is a joke.

00:09:48.129 --> 00:09:48.630
Right?

00:09:48.630 --> 00:09:49.506
Oh, my god.

00:09:49.506 --> 00:09:53.009
Beauty won this year's
Student Academy Award.

00:09:53.009 --> 00:09:58.681
[APPLAUSE]

00:09:58.681 --> 00:10:00.141
Oh.

00:10:00.141 --> 00:10:01.476
That's cool.

00:10:01.476 --> 00:10:02.602
Congratulations.

00:10:02.602 --> 00:10:03.895
Nice, thank you very much.

00:10:13.404 --> 00:10:15.698
My film is about
an ironic journey

00:10:15.698 --> 00:10:18.618
through a breathtaking
underwater world where

00:10:18.618 --> 00:10:21.246
nature and plastic becomes one.

00:10:21.246 --> 00:10:25.333
The story is important to me
because nature gave me so much.

00:10:25.333 --> 00:10:28.378
It's so important to
keep nature alive.

00:10:28.378 --> 00:10:32.215
I want the audience take
away some sensibility about,

00:10:32.215 --> 00:10:33.716
can you use plastic?

00:10:33.716 --> 00:10:35.802
Hope they change
their mind a bit.

00:10:35.802 --> 00:10:38.763
During this production,
I was, like, in a tunnel.

00:10:38.763 --> 00:10:43.434
Reaching this unreachable
Oscar is so amazing.

00:10:43.434 --> 00:10:46.146
It's like landing on
the moon or something.

00:10:46.146 --> 00:10:49.190
Dear Academy, thank you
so much for choosing

00:10:49.190 --> 00:10:50.483
The Beauty as a winner.

00:10:50.483 --> 00:10:53.278
I want to thank also my
entire team for supporting me

00:10:53.278 --> 00:10:54.320
on this crazy journey.

00:10:54.320 --> 00:10:57.073
And I also want to thank
the entire Film Academy

00:10:57.073 --> 00:10:59.659
Baden-Wurttemberg and the
Institute of Animation.

00:10:59.659 --> 00:11:01.286
And last but not
least, I want to thank

00:11:01.286 --> 00:11:04.205
my wife and my kids
who give me strength

00:11:04.205 --> 00:11:06.082
and hope to wrap
it all together.

00:11:06.082 --> 00:11:07.250
Thank you so much.

00:11:07.250 --> 00:11:08.042
Bye, bye.

00:11:12.005 --> 00:11:15.300
Congratulations to
our animation winners.

00:11:15.300 --> 00:11:16.593
I love animation.

00:11:16.593 --> 00:11:17.719
I really love animation.

00:11:17.719 --> 00:11:22.515
Growing up, I learned a lot
from the great animated films.

00:11:22.515 --> 00:11:24.058
Although until I
was 12, I thought

00:11:24.058 --> 00:11:26.144
all mice had four fingers.

00:11:26.144 --> 00:11:30.690
And now it's time for our
next award categories.

00:11:30.690 --> 00:11:33.818
Alfred Hitchcock said,
in feature films,

00:11:33.818 --> 00:11:36.112
the director is God.

00:11:36.112 --> 00:11:39.699
In documentary films,
God is the director.

00:11:39.699 --> 00:11:41.701
In the hands of
this year's winners,

00:11:41.701 --> 00:11:44.704
conscience and conviction
worked together

00:11:44.704 --> 00:11:47.749
to make a world of
complexity and conflict

00:11:47.749 --> 00:11:49.584
just a little clearer.

00:11:49.584 --> 00:11:53.421
And that's why it
was so appropriate

00:11:53.421 --> 00:11:56.674
that they received their good
news from a groundbreaking

00:11:56.674 --> 00:12:00.094
documentarian who most
recently reminded us

00:12:00.094 --> 00:12:03.389
of the power of getting
into good trouble.

00:12:03.389 --> 00:12:04.015
Yeah.

00:12:04.015 --> 00:12:07.936
The film exploring the life
and legacy of John Lewis.

00:12:07.936 --> 00:12:12.565
I'm talking about visionary
producer, director, and writer,

00:12:12.565 --> 00:12:15.360
Dawn Porter.

00:12:15.360 --> 00:12:18.529
I get the honor of telling
you that you have won a medal.

00:12:18.529 --> 00:12:22.325
You are the bronze medalist
in documentary in this year's

00:12:22.325 --> 00:12:23.952
Student Academy Awards.

00:12:23.952 --> 00:12:24.619
Congratulations.

00:12:24.619 --> 00:12:25.620
Thank you so much.

00:12:25.620 --> 00:12:33.086
I didn't expect that,

00:12:33.086 --> 00:12:37.674
Lamya Essemlali is a
French ocean activist.

00:12:37.674 --> 00:12:39.759
I followed her
all over the world

00:12:39.759 --> 00:12:43.846
trying to protect sea turtles
from being poached and whales

00:12:43.846 --> 00:12:47.016
from being hunted.

00:12:47.016 --> 00:12:50.645
Her story shows
everything is possible

00:12:50.645 --> 00:12:53.398
as long as you follow your
ethics and your passion.

00:12:57.652 --> 00:12:59.445
Congratulations.

00:12:59.445 --> 00:13:00.446
Whoo.

00:13:00.446 --> 00:13:01.614
[INAUDIBLE]

00:13:01.614 --> 00:13:03.491
[CHEERING]

00:13:03.491 --> 00:13:05.201
We need to savor this moment.

00:13:07.745 --> 00:13:10.415
I would like people to
really question what is

00:13:10.415 --> 00:13:12.208
my relationship to the ocean.

00:13:12.208 --> 00:13:16.129
To what extent the way I
eat can have an impact?

00:13:18.923 --> 00:13:23.428
I am touched and so appreciative
that the Academy is helping

00:13:23.428 --> 00:13:26.931
your light to shine on
an environmental issue

00:13:26.931 --> 00:13:28.307
to raise awareness.

00:13:28.307 --> 00:13:30.893
Special thanks to my
family and my parents,

00:13:30.893 --> 00:13:34.480
to New York University, to
my very able collaborators,

00:13:34.480 --> 00:13:40.069
to my co-workers and supervisors
from Great Big Story This medal

00:13:40.069 --> 00:13:44.449
is also a recognition for Lemya
Essemlali's determination.

00:13:44.449 --> 00:13:45.783
Thank you from her.

00:13:45.783 --> 00:13:48.828
Thank you, Lamya.

00:13:48.828 --> 00:13:51.998
I have the honor of telling
you that you are a silver medal

00:13:51.998 --> 00:13:55.501
winner in documentary film for
the Academy of Motion Pictures.

00:13:55.501 --> 00:13:57.462
Are you serious?

00:13:57.462 --> 00:13:58.338
I'm serious.

00:14:03.134 --> 00:14:06.512
My film The Dope Years
is about Latasha Harlins

00:14:06.512 --> 00:14:09.724
who was shot and killed
right before the Los Angeles

00:14:09.724 --> 00:14:12.810
uprising in 1991.

00:14:12.810 --> 00:14:15.480
Black in a country
where coming of age

00:14:15.480 --> 00:14:17.648
is coming up far too short.

00:14:17.648 --> 00:14:20.902
Each flower before me represents
those black and brown children

00:14:20.902 --> 00:14:22.904
whose stories were
cut short due to gun

00:14:22.904 --> 00:14:25.948
violence and racial profiling
as a result of playing music too

00:14:25.948 --> 00:14:29.452
loud, for asking for directions,
and in Latasha Harlin's case,

00:14:29.452 --> 00:14:31.287
for buying orange
juice at the age of 15.

00:14:33.915 --> 00:14:35.792
These stories are
still going on.

00:14:35.792 --> 00:14:37.543
I think as filmmakers,
we should always

00:14:37.543 --> 00:14:42.131
strive to not be afraid of
thinking critically and asking

00:14:42.131 --> 00:14:44.634
questions.

00:14:44.634 --> 00:14:46.761
I am a Christian.

00:14:46.761 --> 00:14:50.348
There is this verse that says,
write it out and make it plain.

00:14:50.348 --> 00:14:52.308
One of my goals that
I had written out

00:14:52.308 --> 00:14:57.647
before I went to USC was to
win the Student Academy Award.

00:14:57.647 --> 00:15:01.025
I just recently erased
it from the window.

00:15:01.025 --> 00:15:06.239
That was an incredible feeling.

00:15:06.239 --> 00:15:10.368
To my producers, Menu'ette,
Andreas, and Marquise

00:15:10.368 --> 00:15:15.540
and my Ride or Die 547 team
Fatima, Eka Kevin, Julise,

00:15:15.540 --> 00:15:17.375
Derek, August, and Lawford.

00:15:17.375 --> 00:15:20.503
To my mom and dad, you
mean the world to me.

00:15:20.503 --> 00:15:23.506
This award is dedicated to
activists in Denise Harlins who

00:15:23.506 --> 00:15:25.675
never remain silent.

00:15:25.675 --> 00:15:29.679
Thank you to Ebony and Naomi
for sharing your lives with me.

00:15:29.679 --> 00:15:31.931
Thank you to the USC
school's cinematic arts

00:15:31.931 --> 00:15:34.058
for seeing the
promise in this story.

00:15:34.058 --> 00:15:37.812
And thank you to the Academy
for this honor and recognition.

00:15:37.812 --> 00:15:40.606
This is for Latasha.

00:15:40.606 --> 00:15:42.275
I have the honor
of telling you you

00:15:42.275 --> 00:15:45.111
are the gold medal winner
in documentary this year.

00:15:48.406 --> 00:15:49.782
Really?

00:15:49.782 --> 00:15:51.117
Congratulations.

00:15:54.120 --> 00:15:56.706
My film is about a
Chinese student Xinran

00:15:56.706 --> 00:16:02.253
Li who was beaten to death five
years ago near the USC campus.

00:16:02.253 --> 00:16:05.798
And our main subject,
Rose Tsai, she's

00:16:05.798 --> 00:16:09.260
trying to fight for his justice.

00:16:09.260 --> 00:16:12.054
we are doing this
film to bring change.

00:16:12.054 --> 00:16:14.599
This kind of crime should
never happen again.

00:16:18.728 --> 00:16:21.314
It's very hard to
remain objective

00:16:21.314 --> 00:16:25.234
because I can feel so much
pain with Xinran's parents

00:16:25.234 --> 00:16:26.861
and everyone in the story.

00:16:26.861 --> 00:16:29.530
That's also why I called
it Unfinished Lives

00:16:29.530 --> 00:16:36.329
because I feel for everyone,
their lives is just unfinished.

00:16:36.329 --> 00:16:38.623
I feel like the future
of film is in fantastic

00:16:38.623 --> 00:16:41.542
hands with artists like you.

00:16:41.542 --> 00:16:42.585
Thank you.

00:16:42.585 --> 00:16:44.128
I'm crying.

00:16:44.128 --> 00:16:44.837
OK.

00:16:44.837 --> 00:16:48.049
So I have another news to
announce to all of you.

00:16:48.049 --> 00:16:50.426
We are a winner.

00:16:50.426 --> 00:16:55.514
And we won the gold medal
in the documentary category.

00:16:55.514 --> 00:16:56.015
What?

00:16:59.268 --> 00:17:01.437
We did this film to
honor Xinran's life.

00:17:01.437 --> 00:17:04.982
And I'm very glad that
people care about the story.

00:17:04.982 --> 00:17:08.402
And we got recognized
by the Academy.

00:17:08.402 --> 00:17:13.533
I want to thank Xinran's parents
and Rose and Joel and all

00:17:13.533 --> 00:17:15.535
the subjects we interviewed.

00:17:15.535 --> 00:17:17.662
Not only because they
supported the film

00:17:17.662 --> 00:17:20.831
but also what they
did for the case.

00:17:20.831 --> 00:17:22.500
They made the story happen.

00:17:22.500 --> 00:17:25.544
| also want to thank my
crew and my mentor Amanda.

00:17:25.544 --> 00:17:29.966
They are the best crew and
mentor I can ever ask for.

00:17:29.966 --> 00:17:31.884
I also want to thank my parents.

00:17:31.884 --> 00:17:35.763
Thank you for telling me
that my life is unlimited.

00:17:35.763 --> 00:17:38.641
I love you all.

00:17:38.641 --> 00:17:39.976
How are you?

00:17:39.976 --> 00:17:40.601
I'm good.

00:17:40.601 --> 00:17:41.811
How are you?

00:17:41.811 --> 00:17:45.064
It is my honor to tell you
that you've won the gold medal.

00:17:45.064 --> 00:17:47.775
What?

00:17:47.775 --> 00:17:51.112
You've won the gold medal in
the International Documentary.

00:17:51.112 --> 00:17:51.904
Oh, my god.

00:17:51.904 --> 00:17:53.739
My heart is beating
so fast right now.

00:18:01.205 --> 00:18:03.291
First of all, I would
like to thank Bertha

00:18:03.291 --> 00:18:09.672
for even letting us tell her
story, for opening up to us.

00:18:09.672 --> 00:18:11.465
My film is about Bertha.

00:18:11.465 --> 00:18:15.344
She is an adult now, but
it's about her story,

00:18:15.344 --> 00:18:18.347
a child who had been
abused by her own father.

00:18:18.347 --> 00:18:20.099
[NON-ENGLISH SPEECH]

00:18:29.108 --> 00:18:32.570
The film is about her taking
back the power of her own life,

00:18:32.570 --> 00:18:36.324
not just a story about
experiencing trauma

00:18:36.324 --> 00:18:38.743
and being a victim,
but the power that you

00:18:38.743 --> 00:18:41.912
have within yourself to actually
choose not to be a victim,

00:18:41.912 --> 00:18:44.749
to choose to stand up.

00:18:44.749 --> 00:18:47.168
I wanted to shine a
light on the subject.

00:18:47.168 --> 00:18:49.879
I needed to show the
strength of Bertha,

00:18:49.879 --> 00:18:52.256
strongest woman I've ever met.

00:18:52.256 --> 00:18:55.051
Her story can help a
lot of people in a field

00:18:55.051 --> 00:18:58.387
or in a subjects that people
don't actually talk about.

00:18:58.387 --> 00:19:01.849
I've kind of fallen in
love with documentary

00:19:01.849 --> 00:19:05.478
because you're telling
stories of real people.

00:19:05.478 --> 00:19:08.773
And no matter who you meet
on the street or sit next to

00:19:08.773 --> 00:19:11.442
on a bus, everyone has a story.

00:19:11.442 --> 00:19:13.611
I'm going to say a
sentence that I never

00:19:13.611 --> 00:19:14.862
thought that I would say.

00:19:14.862 --> 00:19:16.572
Thank you to the Academy.

00:19:16.572 --> 00:19:17.073
Bye.

00:19:22.453 --> 00:19:26.749
If documentary films
reveal the world as it is,

00:19:26.749 --> 00:19:30.169
our next category,
alternative experimental,

00:19:30.169 --> 00:19:33.881
invites us into a world as
only the filmmaker can see it.

00:19:33.881 --> 00:19:38.886
From Warhol to Buñuel to
Fassbender and Ackerman they,

00:19:38.886 --> 00:19:42.014
they dare to make
us uncomfortable.

00:19:42.014 --> 00:19:45.810
If John Lewis taught us the
importance of good trouble,

00:19:45.810 --> 00:19:49.271
then this year's alternative
and experimental films

00:19:49.271 --> 00:19:53.776
share with us the good
kind of uncomfortable.

00:19:53.776 --> 00:19:57.571
Shunsaku Hayashi has said
that alternative work

00:19:57.571 --> 00:20:01.242
is like traveling alone
somewhere strange,

00:20:01.242 --> 00:20:05.746
to get lost, disoriented,
and to explore new meanings.

00:20:05.746 --> 00:20:07.790
So I know the
winner was thrilled

00:20:07.790 --> 00:20:11.001
when Shunsaku himself was
kind enough to present

00:20:11.001 --> 00:20:14.088
the medals in this category.

00:20:14.088 --> 00:20:17.925
Your film won the gold medal in
the Alternative or Experimental

00:20:17.925 --> 00:20:18.592
category. .

00:20:18.592 --> 00:20:19.718
Congratulations

00:20:19.718 --> 00:20:21.220
Is this a joke, or is it real?

00:20:21.220 --> 00:20:23.013
You are now a Student
Academy Award winner.

00:20:23.013 --> 00:20:24.265
Oh, my god.

00:20:24.265 --> 00:20:29.812
Our film won the Golden Award
at the Student Academy Oscars.

00:20:29.812 --> 00:20:31.731
You guys are all
Academy award winners.

00:20:31.731 --> 00:20:33.023
[INAUDIBLE]

00:20:33.023 --> 00:20:34.525
You guys are great.

00:20:34.525 --> 00:20:35.359
I'm speechless.

00:20:35.359 --> 00:20:37.319
[LAUGHS]

00:20:37.319 --> 00:20:41.323
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:20:45.327 --> 00:20:50.750
When I work, I'm truly and
fully led by my imagination

00:20:50.750 --> 00:20:52.585
and intuition.

00:20:52.585 --> 00:20:56.672
Simulacra as a concept is
mainly about the connection

00:20:56.672 --> 00:21:01.135
between reality, society,
and symbolism, and also about

00:21:01.135 --> 00:21:04.054
duality.

00:21:04.054 --> 00:21:08.476
It's important for the
whole world in 2020

00:21:08.476 --> 00:21:13.564
because there are a lot of
serious things happening.

00:21:13.564 --> 00:21:17.610
I would want to find a way to
convey this alternative modes

00:21:17.610 --> 00:21:18.569
of storytelling.

00:21:18.569 --> 00:21:23.199
My film is motion capture,
3D scan, 3D motion graphic,

00:21:23.199 --> 00:21:25.576
installations, even
contemporary theater.

00:21:28.370 --> 00:21:31.499
I really want to say big thank
you to my mom and my dad.

00:21:31.499 --> 00:21:34.293
My parents are obviously
super supportive.

00:21:34.293 --> 00:21:37.963
Really want to say big thank
you to all my crew, my friends,

00:21:37.963 --> 00:21:40.841
my colleagues, especially
faculty from USC.

00:21:40.841 --> 00:21:42.968
I'd like to thank the
Academy for bestowing

00:21:42.968 --> 00:21:46.180
this tremendous honor
on our film Simulacra.

00:21:46.180 --> 00:21:49.975
Thank you to the Student
Academy Awards for the award.

00:21:49.975 --> 00:21:50.601
Thanks so much.

00:21:58.484 --> 00:22:02.613
The Student Academy Awards
are the best recognition

00:22:02.613 --> 00:22:05.533
you can expect if you
are a student filmmaker.

00:22:05.533 --> 00:22:07.326
It's the thing that
everybody wants to win.

00:22:07.326 --> 00:22:09.036
It's the Oscars of students.

00:22:12.122 --> 00:22:12.665
I don't know.

00:22:12.665 --> 00:22:14.500
I keep hearing
the word medalist.

00:22:14.500 --> 00:22:16.710
So I guess it
looks like a medal.

00:22:16.710 --> 00:22:19.630
Is it like a stick
figure with its arms

00:22:19.630 --> 00:22:20.965
out and maybe like wings?

00:22:20.965 --> 00:22:21.507
I don't know.

00:22:21.507 --> 00:22:22.424
It's hard to describe it.

00:22:22.424 --> 00:22:23.175
It's like a box.

00:22:26.387 --> 00:22:30.349
I wish the Student Academy
Award would be a small version

00:22:30.349 --> 00:22:31.475
of the real Oscar.

00:22:31.475 --> 00:22:33.227
Like, a little
student with glasses

00:22:33.227 --> 00:22:38.816
on it and a bad haircut, making
this pose, don't have money.

00:22:38.816 --> 00:22:40.776
Something like that
would be really great.

00:22:43.779 --> 00:22:48.367
The biggest lesson, my
god, too many lessons.

00:22:48.367 --> 00:22:49.577
It was exhausting.

00:22:49.577 --> 00:22:52.830
Don't animate when you're sick.

00:22:52.830 --> 00:22:54.331
Just don't do it.

00:22:54.331 --> 00:22:56.542
One of the most important
things that I learned

00:22:56.542 --> 00:22:58.419
is to trust your gut.

00:22:58.419 --> 00:22:59.587
You know what's best.

00:22:59.587 --> 00:23:01.005
It's your story.

00:23:01.005 --> 00:23:03.132
Nobody knows it like you do.

00:23:03.132 --> 00:23:04.216
Just do it.

00:23:04.216 --> 00:23:09.179
You're going to fail, but
when you fail, you will grow.

00:23:09.179 --> 00:23:12.141
That I was going to win
a Student Academy Award.

00:23:12.141 --> 00:23:16.687
Because it was going to give
me more confidence in what

00:23:16.687 --> 00:23:17.605
I was doing.

00:23:17.605 --> 00:23:20.774
I wish I knew that the
first cut of the film

00:23:20.774 --> 00:23:22.401
was going to be one hour long.

00:23:22.401 --> 00:23:26.071
Perhaps, I could have
written a few more scenes.

00:23:26.071 --> 00:23:28.198
And I would've made
a feature out of it.

00:23:28.198 --> 00:23:31.493
If you fly a drone
very close to a river

00:23:31.493 --> 00:23:33.203
it will get sucked down.

00:23:33.203 --> 00:23:34.788
And you will lose it.

00:23:34.788 --> 00:23:38.292
And it will cost a lot of money.

00:23:38.292 --> 00:23:41.420
I recently watched Stanley
Kubrick's Eyes Wide Shut.

00:23:41.420 --> 00:23:44.340
There was a guy who had a
totally different idea of how

00:23:44.340 --> 00:23:45.799
to wear masks.

00:23:45.799 --> 00:23:50.763
It was Kubrick who said a
narrative film should be more

00:23:50.763 --> 00:23:53.933
like music than like fiction.

00:23:53.933 --> 00:23:57.144
It should be a progression
of moods and feelings.

00:23:57.144 --> 00:24:01.565
The theme, what's behind the
emotion, all that comes later.

00:24:01.565 --> 00:24:03.442
This year, student
winners take us

00:24:03.442 --> 00:24:06.153
inside the moods and
feelings of characters

00:24:06.153 --> 00:24:10.783
that command our attention
and capture our affection.

00:24:10.783 --> 00:24:13.285
And like the best
conductors, they

00:24:13.285 --> 00:24:16.330
lead the orchestra
to a crescendo

00:24:16.330 --> 00:24:20.501
and leave us on the edge of our
seats waiting for resolution.

00:24:20.501 --> 00:24:22.211
We are hooked.

00:24:22.211 --> 00:24:25.214
Let's first hear from our
international narrative

00:24:25.214 --> 00:24:26.048
winners.

00:24:26.048 --> 00:24:29.009
And let's relive the big
reveal as they learned

00:24:29.009 --> 00:24:32.930
they were winners, receiving the
news from the acclaimed writer

00:24:32.930 --> 00:24:36.475
and director of last year's
Independent Spirit Award

00:24:36.475 --> 00:24:40.771
winning The Farewell, Lulu Wang.

00:24:40.771 --> 00:24:43.857
You are a Student
Academy Award winner.

00:24:43.857 --> 00:24:44.358
Your film--

00:24:44.358 --> 00:24:44.858
What?

00:24:44.858 --> 00:24:49.071
Won the bronze medal in
the international narrative

00:24:49.071 --> 00:24:51.031
category.

00:24:51.031 --> 00:24:52.783
Whoa.

00:24:52.783 --> 00:24:53.909
Thank you so much.

00:24:53.909 --> 00:24:56.620
I wasn't expecting
this to be, like--

00:24:56.620 --> 00:24:58.664
the results right now.

00:24:58.664 --> 00:25:02.584
Like my heart is pounding.

00:25:05.421 --> 00:25:08.173
When they told me, my roommate
which is also my best friend,

00:25:08.173 --> 00:25:10.092
was right next to the door.

00:25:10.092 --> 00:25:12.928
I wanted to open it and
just hug her, hug someone.

00:25:12.928 --> 00:25:14.763
Because you know, you're
just like in Zoom.

00:25:14.763 --> 00:25:16.056
And you want to be with people.

00:25:16.056 --> 00:25:17.391
You want to share the thing.

00:25:21.895 --> 00:25:24.148
[SPEAKING FRENCH]

00:25:30.070 --> 00:25:33.323
[APPLAUSE]

00:25:34.241 --> 00:25:37.244
My name is Marie Mc Court
I went to Institut des Arts

00:25:37.244 --> 00:25:38.579
de Diffusion

00:25:38.579 --> 00:25:41.665
The name of my movie is I was
Still There When You Left Me.

00:25:44.251 --> 00:25:47.171
While her block is
burning, a 7-year-old girl

00:25:47.171 --> 00:25:49.423
is consumed with guilt,
thinking she started

00:25:49.423 --> 00:25:51.175
the fire of her building.

00:25:51.175 --> 00:25:54.470
[SPEAKING FRENCH]

00:25:56.305 --> 00:25:59.933
What I want my audience to
feel is empathy for sure.

00:25:59.933 --> 00:26:04.021
I want them to ask who was
the real culprit of the fire.

00:26:04.021 --> 00:26:06.648
It's obvious that it's not
the little girl's fault.

00:26:06.648 --> 00:26:09.234
And I guess from what
you see in the movie,

00:26:09.234 --> 00:26:11.320
you can find answers.

00:26:11.320 --> 00:26:14.239
I'm this girl from Paris,
studying in Belgium,

00:26:14.239 --> 00:26:16.325
winning an Academy Award.

00:26:16.325 --> 00:26:17.659
It's so hard to process.

00:26:17.659 --> 00:26:21.538
But I think it's a beautiful
way to welcome our generation

00:26:21.538 --> 00:26:23.415
into the film industry.

00:26:23.415 --> 00:26:26.585
And I hope it's going to
inspire girls around me

00:26:26.585 --> 00:26:29.379
to believe that they can
achieve whatever they want.

00:26:29.379 --> 00:26:29.880
Action

00:26:29.880 --> 00:26:33.675
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:26:35.594 --> 00:26:39.556
You are a Student
Academy Award winner.

00:26:39.556 --> 00:26:42.142
Your film has won
the silver medal

00:26:42.142 --> 00:26:45.145
in the international
narrative category.

00:26:45.145 --> 00:26:47.314
No way.

00:26:47.314 --> 00:26:50.859
Wow, this is a lot
to process right now.

00:26:50.859 --> 00:26:52.528
I mean, this is
a great surprise.

00:26:52.528 --> 00:26:54.071
Wow.

00:26:54.071 --> 00:26:54.988
It's big.

00:26:54.988 --> 00:26:59.493
And I just can't wait to tell
my sisters and my parents

00:26:59.493 --> 00:27:00.410
and my teammates.

00:27:00.410 --> 00:27:03.664
You know, it's a lot.

00:27:03.664 --> 00:27:06.917
[INTERPOSING VOICES]

00:27:08.460 --> 00:27:11.338
My name is Percival
Arguero Mendoza.

00:27:11.338 --> 00:27:14.758
I studied film directing
at Centro de Capacitacion

00:27:14.758 --> 00:27:16.301
Cinematografica.

00:27:16.301 --> 00:27:18.512
My film is called Crescendo.

00:27:22.766 --> 00:27:26.687
Crescendo tells the story of
a young violinist who really,

00:27:26.687 --> 00:27:29.064
really wants her
teacher to accept her

00:27:29.064 --> 00:27:31.567
into his prestigious
string quartet.

00:27:31.567 --> 00:27:34.945
But in the process, she
learns that she really

00:27:34.945 --> 00:27:39.032
needs to stay away from anyone
who expects her to do anything

00:27:39.032 --> 00:27:41.451
other than playing music.

00:27:41.451 --> 00:27:45.789
We'd like to think of Crescendo
as a story of empowerment

00:27:45.789 --> 00:27:47.332
in the face of abuse.

00:27:47.332 --> 00:27:50.377
We wanted to be part of
that conversation that

00:27:50.377 --> 00:27:52.004
was long overdue.

00:27:52.004 --> 00:27:54.923
I'm really happy to say
that making Crescendo

00:27:54.923 --> 00:27:58.719
was a delight for me and
my whole team, actually.

00:27:58.719 --> 00:28:01.680
We're very much in love
with each other, you know.

00:28:01.680 --> 00:28:04.308
My team was really there
for me and was really

00:28:04.308 --> 00:28:06.018
there for the project.

00:28:06.018 --> 00:28:10.230
I would really like to thank
my parents and my sisters.

00:28:10.230 --> 00:28:13.567
Of course, I'd like to
thank the cast and the crew.

00:28:13.567 --> 00:28:16.069
Last but not least, I'd
like to thank the Academy

00:28:16.069 --> 00:28:18.280
for this huge recognition.

00:28:18.280 --> 00:28:21.825
The whole team is
excited about it.

00:28:21.825 --> 00:28:24.995
You've won a Student
Academy Award.

00:28:24.995 --> 00:28:26.622
Your film has won--

00:28:26.622 --> 00:28:27.497
Oh, my god.

00:28:27.497 --> 00:28:31.835
The gold medal in the
international narrative

00:28:31.835 --> 00:28:33.003
category.

00:28:33.003 --> 00:28:36.340
Very well deserved
and congratulations.

00:28:36.340 --> 00:28:41.303
Well, I need some
second to process this.

00:28:41.303 --> 00:28:44.014
Now if I'm going to tell
my team that this happened,

00:28:44.014 --> 00:28:46.058
in my school they're
going to all freak out.

00:28:46.058 --> 00:28:48.310
Oh.

00:28:48.310 --> 00:28:50.938
Bravo.

00:28:50.938 --> 00:28:51.647
I'm German Galub.

00:28:51.647 --> 00:28:53.982
And I'm from Estonia,
from Tallinn.

00:28:53.982 --> 00:28:57.027
And I study in Baltic
Film Media School.

00:28:57.027 --> 00:28:59.363
And we did the film
My Dear Corpses,

00:28:59.363 --> 00:29:03.992
or in Estonian,
[ESTONIAN] Basically,

00:29:03.992 --> 00:29:08.080
talking about young man who is
desperate to get his home back

00:29:08.080 --> 00:29:09.122
for his mother.

00:29:09.122 --> 00:29:14.044
And he will start to work
as a corpse transporter.

00:29:20.050 --> 00:29:23.095
I worked myself as a
corpse transporter.

00:29:23.095 --> 00:29:24.680
So I know this work.

00:29:24.680 --> 00:29:26.932
And this film is
not autobiographical

00:29:26.932 --> 00:29:29.142
or some biopic in a sense.

00:29:29.142 --> 00:29:31.061
I just had some experience.

00:29:31.061 --> 00:29:33.814
And I thought what
would happen if I'm

00:29:33.814 --> 00:29:36.149
going to push it a
little bit further

00:29:36.149 --> 00:29:40.445
and tell something important
that I understand personally.

00:29:40.445 --> 00:29:42.239
It's a rite of passage.

00:29:42.239 --> 00:29:45.158
I tried to give people
opportunity to say

00:29:45.158 --> 00:29:46.702
that we are not immortal.

00:29:46.702 --> 00:29:48.704
And we have to remember that.

00:29:48.704 --> 00:29:53.417
We have to remember what is
human, what makes us human.

00:29:53.417 --> 00:29:55.419
That's the most important thing.

00:29:55.419 --> 00:29:58.005
I would like to
recognize for my win.

00:29:58.005 --> 00:30:02.050
But I wouldn't say my win
because a lot of people

00:30:02.050 --> 00:30:02.676
did this.

00:30:02.676 --> 00:30:05.387
And they were so
dedicated to the project

00:30:05.387 --> 00:30:08.015
and that made a
whole difference.

00:30:08.015 --> 00:30:11.810
If it's done from the heart,
then it's done the best way.

00:30:11.810 --> 00:30:16.189
On this project worked
over 90 people for you all.

00:30:16.189 --> 00:30:20.777
Thank you again, [ESTONIAN].

00:30:20.777 --> 00:30:24.740
And now, our final award of
the evening, the 2020 Student

00:30:24.740 --> 00:30:27.576
Academy Awards for
domestic narrative.

00:30:27.576 --> 00:30:29.369
Just imagine how
thrilling it was

00:30:29.369 --> 00:30:33.332
for them to have their Zoom
interrupted by a living

00:30:33.332 --> 00:30:36.793
legend, not just of the
Academy but also of the Student

00:30:36.793 --> 00:30:37.919
Academy.

00:30:37.919 --> 00:30:42.883
Listen, in 1983, the Academy
bestowed a student award

00:30:42.883 --> 00:30:46.345
on a 26-year-old for
his master's thesis.

00:30:46.345 --> 00:30:50.098
Well, 37 years later, he's
still breaking new grounds

00:30:50.098 --> 00:30:52.726
with the Da 5 Bloods.

00:30:52.726 --> 00:30:57.439
We were honored that the
inimitable filmmaker and Oscar

00:30:57.439 --> 00:31:02.235
winner Spike Lee joined us
to pass the creative torch.

00:31:02.235 --> 00:31:04.863
Basically, I'm on this call--

00:31:04.863 --> 00:31:05.363
Hello.

00:31:05.363 --> 00:31:06.990
Hello, Talia.

00:31:06.990 --> 00:31:08.033
How are you?

00:31:08.033 --> 00:31:08.533
I'm good.

00:31:08.533 --> 00:31:09.076
How are you?

00:31:09.076 --> 00:31:09.993
And then they
asked me, you know,

00:31:09.993 --> 00:31:11.495
what it feels like
being a finalist.

00:31:11.495 --> 00:31:13.872
It's always a very
romantic idea to be

00:31:13.872 --> 00:31:15.373
recognized by the Academy.

00:31:15.373 --> 00:31:18.585
And they say to me, we
have a surprise for you.

00:31:18.585 --> 00:31:20.921
I've invited a special
guest to join us.

00:31:20.921 --> 00:31:24.299
And then Spike Lee pops up.

00:31:24.299 --> 00:31:25.300
Hey!

00:31:25.300 --> 00:31:28.428
How are you, Spike?

00:31:28.428 --> 00:31:29.679
Is this happening?

00:31:29.679 --> 00:31:30.180
Is this--

00:31:30.180 --> 00:31:32.682
It's not Industrial
Light and Magic.

00:31:32.682 --> 00:31:33.266
It's me.

00:31:33.266 --> 00:31:35.852
I was like, what?

00:31:35.852 --> 00:31:37.729
It's such an honor
to meet you, Spike.

00:31:37.729 --> 00:31:39.022
Like I--

00:31:39.022 --> 00:31:41.024
Rommel, the honor's mine.

00:31:41.024 --> 00:31:46.863
I'm here to tell you,
you won the bronze award.

00:31:46.863 --> 00:31:48.198
Wow.

00:31:48.198 --> 00:31:49.783
You did it, my man.

00:31:49.783 --> 00:31:52.369
[SPEAKING SPANISH]

00:32:00.377 --> 00:32:01.878
Congrats, Rommel.

00:32:01.878 --> 00:32:02.754
That's amazing.

00:32:05.465 --> 00:32:07.175
My name is Rommel Villa.

00:32:07.175 --> 00:32:09.845
I went to the University
of Southern California.

00:32:09.845 --> 00:32:11.930
And my film is Sweet Potatoes.

00:32:11.930 --> 00:32:17.602
My film is a historical
film about Luis Miramontes,

00:32:17.602 --> 00:32:20.856
the Mexican scientist who
synthesized the birth control

00:32:20.856 --> 00:32:21.356
pill.

00:32:21.356 --> 00:32:25.235
This story basically focuses
on the religious, personal,

00:32:25.235 --> 00:32:28.905
and socio-econoomical
consequences of his invention.

00:32:28.905 --> 00:32:30.740
It definitely feels great.

00:32:30.740 --> 00:32:33.952
It's a huge honor
to have this win.

00:32:33.952 --> 00:32:35.495
There have been
many times where I

00:32:35.495 --> 00:32:37.122
didn't believe I was talented.

00:32:37.122 --> 00:32:39.958
There are a lot of, I
think, confidence struggles

00:32:39.958 --> 00:32:41.918
that filmmakers go through.

00:32:41.918 --> 00:32:47.382
So getting this win, it means,
I think it means everything.

00:32:47.382 --> 00:32:48.842
First of all, I
would like to thank

00:32:48.842 --> 00:32:50.635
God for the blessings
and challenges

00:32:50.635 --> 00:32:52.220
that life has given to me.

00:32:52.220 --> 00:32:54.139
Thank you so much to my
family, to my partner,

00:32:54.139 --> 00:32:56.474
and to my friends for believing
in me, especially when I

00:32:56.474 --> 00:32:57.642
don't believe in myself.

00:32:57.642 --> 00:32:59.769
This film was made
with the heart and soul

00:32:59.769 --> 00:33:05.400
of a lot of minorities who put
their passion into filmmaking.

00:33:05.400 --> 00:33:09.196
Congratulations. you
won the silver award.

00:33:12.866 --> 00:33:14.117
Oh, my God.

00:33:14.117 --> 00:33:15.535
[INAUDIBLE]

00:33:15.535 --> 00:33:17.120
I know I got a call yesterday.

00:33:17.120 --> 00:33:19.664
And I found out that I won
the Student Academy Award.

00:33:19.664 --> 00:33:20.540
What?

00:33:20.540 --> 00:33:22.751
Wow.

00:33:22.751 --> 00:33:27.088
I'm Kareshma Dube from New York
University, Grad Film Program.

00:33:27.088 --> 00:33:29.382
And my film's called Bittu.

00:33:29.382 --> 00:33:33.720
[NON-ENGLISH SPEECH]

00:33:36.139 --> 00:33:39.059
At it's heart,
it's an examination

00:33:39.059 --> 00:33:42.187
of a very close friendship
between two girls.

00:33:42.187 --> 00:33:44.898
I put in a lot of my
personal experiences

00:33:44.898 --> 00:33:47.776
going to school in
India in there as well.

00:33:47.776 --> 00:33:51.112
It's inspired a lot by my own
relationship with authority.

00:33:51.112 --> 00:33:55.158
A lot of my work stems from
the idea of individuality

00:33:55.158 --> 00:33:57.661
and the cost that comes
with it, especially

00:33:57.661 --> 00:34:00.830
in like traditional societies.

00:34:00.830 --> 00:34:04.042
Winning a Student Academy
Award means everything

00:34:04.042 --> 00:34:06.378
really at this
point in my career.

00:34:06.378 --> 00:34:08.463
I think the validation
is very important.

00:34:08.463 --> 00:34:10.840
Romantically, any film
maker anywhere in the world

00:34:10.840 --> 00:34:13.552
would love to be
recognized by the Academy.

00:34:13.552 --> 00:34:16.096
It, kind of, puts a
lot of life and energy

00:34:16.096 --> 00:34:18.598
into what you really
need to keep going.

00:34:18.598 --> 00:34:21.685
I'd like to thank my
amazing, strong, wonderfully

00:34:21.685 --> 00:34:24.354
strange family back home in
India who are responsible

00:34:24.354 --> 00:34:26.106
for me being here today.

00:34:26.106 --> 00:34:28.775
I'd never made a film before
going into the NYU Grad Film

00:34:28.775 --> 00:34:29.401
Program.

00:34:29.401 --> 00:34:30.986
And I learned it
all from my friends.

00:34:30.986 --> 00:34:32.988
So I'd like to thank
each and every person

00:34:32.988 --> 00:34:34.447
on the team of Bittu.

00:34:34.447 --> 00:34:37.284
Your work has
transformed the movie.

00:34:37.284 --> 00:34:39.661
Congratulations.

00:34:39.661 --> 00:34:41.329
Thank you so much.

00:34:41.329 --> 00:34:43.123
You've won the gold.

00:34:46.584 --> 00:34:49.838
Gold is the top prize.

00:34:49.838 --> 00:34:55.593
Spike Lee told me
that we got gold.

00:34:55.593 --> 00:34:56.261
That Umama--

00:34:59.180 --> 00:35:01.850
[NON-ENGLISH SPEECH]

00:35:10.609 --> 00:35:13.611
It's a story that a
lot of South Africans

00:35:13.611 --> 00:35:18.366
can relate to from different
perspectives within the film.

00:35:18.366 --> 00:35:20.994
And I think it's important
for South Africans

00:35:20.994 --> 00:35:25.040
to be able to see
themselves but also to see

00:35:25.040 --> 00:35:27.584
the other people who they
have relationships with

00:35:27.584 --> 00:35:29.461
and see their perspectives.

00:35:29.461 --> 00:35:32.213
Thank you so much
to the Academy.

00:35:32.213 --> 00:35:35.342
You made the dreams of
a little 8-year-old girl

00:35:35.342 --> 00:35:37.385
from South Africa come true.

00:35:37.385 --> 00:35:39.262
Just to be nominated
is phenomenal.

00:35:39.262 --> 00:35:42.641
Why am I crying?

00:35:42.641 --> 00:35:46.186
Thank you, NYU, for
championing this film.

00:35:46.186 --> 00:35:50.065
My incredible producing
team, my incredible cast

00:35:50.065 --> 00:35:53.443
I feel like the film gods
have put you on this earth

00:35:53.443 --> 00:35:55.153
to be in this film.

00:35:55.153 --> 00:36:00.575
I'm standing here as a
really proud South African.

00:36:00.575 --> 00:36:04.704
Because I know that this is
a validation to us all that

00:36:04.704 --> 00:36:07.749
our stories have a
place to be told.

00:36:07.749 --> 00:36:09.959
And we can tell them.

00:36:09.959 --> 00:36:10.627
Thank you.

00:36:17.634 --> 00:36:19.511
Under one hour, huh?

00:36:19.511 --> 00:36:22.305
Maybe they should have
me host the Oscars.

00:36:22.305 --> 00:36:25.141
Well, what a night
this has been,

00:36:25.141 --> 00:36:28.478
watching the look of pure joy
on the faces of our winners

00:36:28.478 --> 00:36:31.773
as the legends they look up
to, share the incredible news

00:36:31.773 --> 00:36:34.651
that so much hard
work has paid off.

00:36:34.651 --> 00:36:38.780
It's just, you know, it's the
look of determination rewarded

00:36:38.780 --> 00:36:41.449
and congratulations
delivered by the very best

00:36:41.449 --> 00:36:44.494
in the field to the
best yet to come.

00:36:44.494 --> 00:36:47.414
All those late nights,
so many dreams,

00:36:47.414 --> 00:36:50.500
and so much daring,
and so much hope

00:36:50.500 --> 00:36:53.753
reaffirmed and renewed about
the promise and potential

00:36:53.753 --> 00:36:56.214
that stretches out still ahead.

00:36:56.214 --> 00:37:01.386
Admittedly, 2020 has given
us all reason to drink.

00:37:01.386 --> 00:37:02.679
Right?

00:37:02.679 --> 00:37:06.933
But tonight, tonight has given
us reason to lift the glass

00:37:06.933 --> 00:37:09.769
and raise a toast.

00:37:09.769 --> 00:37:12.522
To the time when we can
all be together again

00:37:12.522 --> 00:37:17.652
in person, to big dreams,
and to a hopeful tomorrow.

00:37:17.652 --> 00:37:18.820
Cheers.

00:37:18.820 --> 00:37:20.238
Buena noches.

00:37:20.238 --> 00:37:20.864
Y buena suerte.

00:37:26.244 --> 00:37:28.663
This is water.

00:37:28.663 --> 00:37:30.498
Come on.

